## Ownership Boundary

The OCaml guardian owns the PTY master, child process, guardian socket, durable
output log, custody journal, and physical generation descriptor. The OCaml
kernel owns the public client socket, semantic journal, input lease, output
reconciliation, and coordination endpoint. TUI and GUI processes own no agent
lifecycle authority. Closing a client releases only its observer connection or
input lease; killing the kernel closes every client but not the PTY generation.

The C boundary contains only `forkpty` and terminal window-size calls. It owns
no session state, protocol transition, retry policy, journal, or client logic.

## Beagle Product Authority

The Beagle integration is a product composition, not a repository merger. Each
layer retains exactly one authority:

| Surface | Owns | Must not own |
|---|---|---|
| Sounio | semantic contracts, executable verifiers, bounded recovery receipts | UI session state, raw terminal retention, Beagle canonical memory |
| Loom OCaml runtime | PTY Guardian, child process, physical generation, input lease, durable byte journal, replay cursor, kernel recovery | agent routing, memory curation, UI rendering |
| Beagle Workspace Agent | agent registry, routing, logical sessions/panes, command blocks, provenance, redaction, memory-import policy | PTY custody, process identity, recovery fencing |
| Project Cockpit | authenticated product gateway and operator UI | fallback PTY authority or an independent mutable session ledger |
| Warp Workbench | optional renderer behind its existing license boundary | runtime or memory authority |
| Beagle Core | curated canonical memory and durable knowledge graph | live leases, process liveness, terminal consensus, raw output by default |
| Kubernetes | scheduling, storage attachment, failure injection, Pod replacement | lane identity or proof of semantic continuity |

Run the compatibility backend beside Beagle Workspace Agent:

```bash
bin/sounio-loom beagle-serve \
  --bind 127.0.0.1 \
  --port 4372 \
  --state-dir /workspace/.beagle/loom
```

The Workspace Agent selects it through its existing configuration:

```text
BEAGLE_PTY_SUPERVISOR_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4372
```

The bridge implements the existing internal `beagle-pty-supervisor-v1`
contract without changing the external `beagle-terminal-v1` protocol:

```text
GET  /health
GET  /v1/health
GET  /v1/panes
POST /v1/spawn
GET  /v1/panes/:paneId/snapshot
POST /v1/panes/:paneId/input
POST /v1/panes/:paneId/resize
POST /v1/panes/:paneId/signal
POST /v1/panes/:paneId/terminate
WS   /v1/panes/:paneId/stream
```

Original response fields are preserved. The additive Loom evidence fields are:

```text
supervisorRuntime
supervisorProtocol
loomInstanceId
loomKernelPid
loomGuardianPid
loomState
loomCursor
generationFingerprint
authorityStatus.journalVerified
authorityStatus.semanticJournalHead
authorityStatus.guardianJournalHead
authorityStatus.kernelRecoveryCount
authorityStatus.lineageVerified
authorityStatus.generationLineageHead
authorityStatus.generationTransition
authorityStatus.generationTransitionCount
authorityStatus.podResurrectionCount
authorityStatus.predecessorInstanceId
authorityStatus.predecessorSemanticJournalHead
authorityStatus.predecessorGuardianJournalHead
```

Beagle's `paneId` is encoded injectively and reversibly as the Loom lane:

```text
agent = beagle-workbench
lane  = pane-<lowercase hexadecimal UTF-8 paneId>
session_id = Beagle sessionId
```

The bridge refuses any persisted pane when its requested `sessionId` or
canonical `cwd` differs from persisted identity, including after total process
loss. Kernel recovery
must preserve instance, Guardian, harness, and generation fingerprint. Starting
after a terminated or lost generation must create a new instance and
fingerprint, so a replacement shell cannot be presented as the old process.

`generationFingerprint` is a stable local generation identifier derived from
pane, session, instance, process-birth identity, and command digest. It is not a
signature. Journal heads plus `journalVerified=true` are executable local
evidence; they become a recovery receipt only after a named Sounio verifier
binds them to a requested transition and evidence policy.

When the predecessor journals end in `Active` but none of their recorded
process-birth identities remain, a successor is classified `pod-resurrected`.
When the semantic journal ends in `Exited`, it is `clean-respawn`. Each
transition appends and fsyncs `beagle.lineage.tsv`; its hash-chain payload binds
pane, session, transition class, predecessor, successor, and both predecessor
journal heads. Every prior predecessor journal is reverified when the lineage
is read. A broken chain, generation gap, phase mismatch, metadata mismatch, or
mutated predecessor journal refuses the next generation before spawn.

Raw terminal output is not imported into Beagle Core automatically. The
Workspace Agent's secret detection, block folding, privacy class, and
assisted-import policy remain the only path from runtime evidence to canonical
memory.

The bridge binds to loopback by default. A non-loopback bind requires explicit
`--allow-remote`; Loom capabilities and Unix sockets are never returned through
HTTP; bodies, identities, terminal dimensions, and signals are bounded. The
WebSocket SHA-1 operation is required by RFC 6455 only and is not used as an
integrity or identity primitive.

The first deployment must be a shadow/canary workspace with the Node supervisor
and Loom on different loopback ports. Only one backend may own a Workspace Agent
instance. Promotion additionally requires deployed API/stream compatibility,
block provenance and redaction parity, API and kernel failure, and Pod deletion
in a shadow namespace. Before changing the live workspace, reconcile the image
source branch and the StatefulSet update strategy. The Node supervisor and
Cockpit-local mutation fallback remain compatibility paths until that canary
passes; they are not co-authorities.

## Durable Protocol

Each session uses an authenticated local `LOOM/1` Unix-socket protocol:

```text
STATUS
SNAPSHOT(cursor, limit) -> bytes[cursor..nextCursor)
ATTACH(Observe, cursor) -> replay(cursor) + live bytes
ATTACH(Interactive, cursor, InputLease) -> replay(cursor) + live bytes + input
RESIZE(cols, rows) -> Guardian terminal geometry + dual-journal event
SIGNAL(SIGINT | SIGTERM | SIGHUP) -> bounded Guardian delivery + dual-journal event
STOP -> terminal journal event
```

The replaceable kernel holds one authenticated `GUARD/1` full-duplex bridge:

```text
STATUS -> guardian identity, harness identity, durable cursor, bridge count
STREAM(cursor) -> fsynced replay(cursor) + live bytes + process input
RESIZE(cols, rows) -> PTY window size + custody event
SIGNAL(SIGINT | SIGTERM | SIGHUP) -> terminal input or harness signal + custody event
STOP -> guardian terminates the harness and records GUARDIAN_EXITED
```

The guardian appends and fsyncs output before its custody `OUTPUT` event and
before forwarding bytes to the kernel. The kernel records semantic `OUTPUT`
events before client broadcast. After kernel loss, `recover` compares the last
semantic cursor with the guardian cursor and appends one `OUTPUT_RECONCILED`
event for the exact missing range before accepting clients.

The journal verifier checks both its contiguous SHA-256 chain and the semantic
state machine. In particular, `LEASE_ACQUIRED` is legal only while the session
is active and no other lease exists; `LEASE_RELEASED` must name the exact holder;
`KERNEL_RECOVERED` explicitly clears leases whose sockets died with the old
kernel; session exit requires no live input lease; and no event is legal after
exit. A separate guardian verifier checks its hash chain, output continuity,
and terminal custody state.

## Migration Boundary

The existing Python agent supervisor remains unchanged as a compatibility path
for already-running fleet generations. Loom-1.4 starts new native Loom sessions
without that supervisor, and the shared coordinator can validate and wake those
sessions through `transport=loom`. Migrating a live fleet slot requires a later
gate that preserves the slot's generation identity, coordination endpoint,
recovery budget, HOME isolation, and pending inbox delivery. The local
three-generation replay gate is necessary but not sufficient for that live
migration. Until a separate-Pod gate exists, a working legacy generation is
intentionally not restarted merely to demonstrate the new UI.

The Beagle bridge is a separate migration edge. It preserves the Workspace
Agent's current internal supervisor API while replacing its process authority.
Only one supervisor backend may serve a Workspace Agent instance. Project
Cockpit and Warp remain clients of `beagle-terminal-v1`; Beagle Core remains a
curated memory authority rather than a lease or liveness store.

## Falsification Conditions

- killing all observers or the GUI changes the harness PID;
- killing the kernel changes the guardian, harness, or instance identity;
- starting a replacement generation while its recoverable Guardian is active rotates the capability or changes custody;
- output produced while no kernel exists is absent after recovery;
- a dead-kernel endpoint remains active or a recovered endpoint changes generation;
- an unacknowledged wake is suppressed because a successor reused the lane's endpoint ID;
- a retry injects twice into one verified generation, or a durable ACK replays into a later generation;
- a persisted descriptor whose kernel and Guardian birth identities are absent is displayed as active;
- recovery preserves an input lease whose owning client died with the kernel;
- two kernels acquire the guardian bridge;
- a second interactive client acquires input authority;
- reconnecting at a valid cursor omits or duplicates output bytes;
- a cursor beyond durable output is accepted;
- output is broadcast before it is durably appended;
- a rehashed duplicate-lease journal verifies;
- an observer can write input or the GUI exposes a mutation endpoint;
- two Beagle supervisor backends own the same logical pane;
- a Beagle reconnect changes instance, Guardian, harness, or generation
  fingerprint after kernel-only loss;
- a terminated Beagle pane respawns with the previous instance or fingerprint;
- a Beagle identity or cwd conflict is accepted after generation loss;
- a successor is spawned before its predecessor journals and lineage verify;
- a clean exit is reported as Pod resurrection, or an active unclean predecessor
  is reported as a clean respawn;
- a lineage receipt verifies after any linked predecessor journal is mutated;
- a Beagle WebSocket cursor moves backwards or changes instance mid-stream;
- the terminal journal state verifies while an input lease remains active;
- the attach path requires an external terminal multiplexer.

## Evidence Boundary

Loom-1.6 proves client- and kernel-failure independence on one host,
proof-linked logical continuity after total local process loss, and bounded
at-least-once inbox replay across four Kubernetes Pod generations. The real
canary relocated compute from t560-proxmox to r740-proxmox while one Ceph RBD
RWOP PVC retained state. This is not state replication: RWOP fenced concurrent
writers, no independent replica was reconciled, and no network partition or
consensus case was exercised.

Each generation produced a canonical Ed25519 certificate over native-Sounio
facts and verdict. The retained certificates verify offline after Pod, PVC, and
Secret cleanup. Sounio now makes signed resurrection a distinct private proof
type: an unsigned verified proof cannot be passed to signed promotion, and
ordinary code cannot construct the signed proof. The signer was nevertheless
co-located with the workload. Its signature proves integrity and keyholder
authorship, not correctness against a faulty or compromised signing authority.
The correct-signature/wrong-facts experiment exposed the load-bearing gap: a
legitimate key can sign false facts and reach signed typestate admission. An
independent observer can now precommit the exact receipt digest; the tested
post-observation rewrite is refused before `start_command`. Sounio represents
decision, observation, and principal disjointness as private nominal proof
terms. The host derives both principal tokens from canonical Ed25519 public-key
DER, so changing only PEM serialization cannot manufacture independence. This
establishes typed role separation and bounded tamper detection.
It does not establish semantic truth when both authorities accept an initially
false fact, nor prove organizational, process, or hardware independence. Those
claims require observation semantics, separate custody, and collusion tests.

The PTY still depends on one Guardian process: abrupt Guardian or host loss
cannot re-adopt the existing PTY. The retained PVC remains a single durability
dependency. The output log is intentionally unbounded so cursor replay remains
lossless; retention and compaction require an acknowledged-checkpoint protocol.
The witnesses do not cover canonical-memory import, deployed Cockpit/Warp
rendering, production authority, Madaros linear parity, exactly-once external
effects, storage loss, Byzantine behavior, PL/CS novelty, or priority.
